~ start_visit_1
Office Worker: Get me a beer, please.
- Bartender: How are you feeling, friend?
	Office Worker: I don't wanna talk about it. I just wanna drick, okay?
- Bartender: Seems like you need something stronger than a beer.
	Office Worker: You got that right. Doesn't mean that I should have it.
- Bartender: Curse this blasted weather am I right?
	Office Worker: Does it look like I care about some goddamn rain or poor air quality?
Office Worker: Sorry. I don’t have any right to take out any frustration I have onto you. It’s been a really long day at the office. Never-ending mountains of paperwork and people that nag about me needing to work harder.
- Bartender: We all have those days friend. Just a part of life.
	Office Worker: I don’t remember when the days turned into months. Can’t tell any difference between the nagging from my boss or my wife, it all sounds the same now.
- Bartender: You seem to be a man that cares considering how much you allow yourself to suffer.
	Office Worker: Cares huh? Being a decent person doesn’t get you very far anymore. Always stuck with no way to get out.
- Bartender: If you have to binge paperwork you might as well binge booze too.
	Office Worker: Don’t know if you want to be friendly bartender or just a slimy salesman…
Office Worker: You know what. I need something better than beer. Got something in mind?
Bartender: One relaxing tonic coming right up. => END!

~ after_drink_1
Office Worker: Thanks for the drink, friend. => END!


~ start_visit_2
Office Worker: Looks like you’re still behind the same counter. Seems like we share the curse of being stuck in the same goddamn place.
- Bartender: Just doing my part. There’s will be a reward for those that contribute.
	Office Worker: Yeah like what? A nice house? A loving wife? That’s a whole lotta shit you are spewing “Friend”.
- Bartender: You should always have a Friend nearby when you need it.
	Office Worker: But I shouldn’t need it. Here we were taught that our lives was gonna get better with time. What a fucking lie that was.
Office Worker: As a bartender I bet you hear some funny stories… Get ready for one. After slaving away for years behind my goddamn desk, never doing anything else but eat and sleep… How does my loving wife reward me? By fucking the car salesman living right next door. That’s right, found them humping like bunnies in the garage a few hours ago. She complains that I’m never home or never earn enough money. Meanwhile she goes and fucks that skinny jeans-wearing fuck even though his life is exactly the same as mine. She doesn’t give a rats ass about how much I’ve sacrificed for her! For Us!
- Bartender: See seems like a bad Friend.
	Office Worker: Friend this, Friend that. Like it ever mattered. Especially to her.
- Bartender: I hear your pain, but you know anger isn’t acceptable.
	Office Worker: Fuck what’s acceptable!! This is how I feel jackass! Not you or anyone else can dictate that. Especially her!
Office Worker: I swear next time I see her… I’ll make her pay… I’ll make her suffer for all the suffering she brought me… Making me think we had a shot in this godforsaken world. If only I had “worked harder”. She could try doing what I do. She’s weak. She’s fragile. She would fall over in an instance…
Bartender: Hey Friend, before you go and do that, let me pour you a drink on the house. It’s a special recipe of mine. It’s been known to make any extreme emotion feel less… damaging. If you have that and still feel the need to make her pay… Well we’ll cross that bridge when we get there.
Office Worker: Free drink huh. Fine, if you think it will help then make it.
Bartender: Trust me I know it will. => END!

~ after_drink_2
Office Worker: I feel…[wait=1.4] I…[wait=0.8] I don’t know how I feel…[wait=1.0] Sorry, I need to go…[wait=0.45] Thanks for the drink… => END!


~ start_visit_3
Office Worker: She’s gone…[wait=1.0]
Bartender:…
Office Worker: She left with the car salesman. Note said that she was moving to a new sector with him. Doesn’t matter if it’s one for housing Elite or near the Warzone, she’s deluded herself that she can be happy with someone else. The only thing I know for sure is that I can’t make her happy. I can’t even make myself happy. This blasted place will never let me.
Bartender: Never lose faith in our Great Friend. Good things never come to those that don’t believe.
Office Worker: I never believed in it. I believed in her. Every time I try to get her out of my head I she keeps yelling at me. An echo that never fades.
Bartender: …Making her go away…[wait=0.5] permanently…[wait=0.8] it’s doable. It’s sounds like something that you need. To become productive again. To be useful in life again.
Office Worker: Huh you can do that with the stuff behind your counter?
Bartender: Just trust your Friendly bartender. => END!

